Steam Enhanced, client/game-page/layout.js: fit() keeps a piece's span while its height is unknown.

Why. fit() wrote every piece's span from the height its ResizeObserver last reported, and 1 for a
piece it has no height for yet. A piece that already had a span (one this pass just observed, a node
Steam made anew, or a span set ahead of the pass) was put back on a single 1px row: it stood over
the pieces under it, and they moved up and back down when its size came. Measured on Dota 2's page in
the main window (visible), with the place each block had on the last visit given to it at once
(overlay/nav.js): the cover and the description fell to span 1 at ~620 ms and got their span back at
~1.8 s; every block of both columns moved 514 / 736 px up and back down in that time. With this
patch: no piece loses its span, no block moves (0 of 15).

What. A piece with no reported height and a span of its own keeps that span; one with no span gets 1
as before (the grid's default, nothing changes for a first pass); a measured height (0 included)
sets the span as before.

--- a/client/game-page/layout.js
+++ b/client/game-page/layout.js
@@ -96,8 +96,11 @@
     live.fitQueued = 0;
     const c = document.querySelector(CONTAINER);
     if (!c) return;
-    const spans = pieces(c).map((el) => [el, heights.get(el) || 0]);
-    for (const [el, h] of spans) {
+    for (const el of pieces(c)) {
+      const h = heights.get(el);
+      // Not measured yet (observed this frame, or a node Steam made anew): a span it already has
+      // stays; writing 1 stood the piece on one row, over the pieces under it, until its size came
+      if (h === undefined && el.style.getPropertyValue('--se-span')) continue;
       setVar(el, '--se-span', String(h > 0 ? Math.ceil((h + GAP) / ROW) : 1));
     }
   }
